WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.419 --> 00:00:05.966
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.966 --> 00:00:11.626
My days as an assistant principal began
by the flagpole, welcoming students

00:00:11.626 --> 00:00:17.660
to school, like Roberto with his
rattail and ninja turtle backpack.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.230
He was just one of those
kiddos who made me smile.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:26.320
I'd greet students with high-fives,
fist bumps and,

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.565
as Roberto taught me, the super
cool sliding handshake fist bump.

00:00:31.565 --> 00:00:34.510
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Then, I would usher them through

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:39.670
the doors, thinking that once we were all
inside, we could get down to the business

00:00:39.670 --> 00:00:44.170
of learning and growing up,
unaffected by the outside world.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
I became a teacher because
of students like Roberto.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.500
I knew that education could open doors.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.870
I learned, however,
that a community can change a school.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:04.580
Today, I want to introduce you
to Mitchell Middle School.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:09.100
I want to introduce you to Mitchell
because it introduced me to challenges

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.800
I still grapple with.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:16.714
It transformed my understanding
of community and education.

00:01:16.714 --> 00:01:21.670
To get to Mitchell, you go to
the trendiest neighborhood in town,

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:25.260
then head just four blocks to the east.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:30.420
The spin studios and
breweries quickly shift to small homes,

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:35.150
vacant lots and
the dollar tree inside the building.

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:40.090
Our students, like most teenagers,
grappled with their first essay,

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.030
saying Selena Gomez at break.

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:46.730
Outside the school, they helped care for

00:01:46.730 --> 00:01:51.440
little siblings worried about
the deportation of a family member.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:59.000
Struggled sometimes to find food
in an area with no grocery store.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:02.580
My kids carry the trauma and

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.750
uncertainties with them that I,
the adult, had never known.

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:16.300
Mitchell taught me that the brick walls of
a school building do not make a fortress.

00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:21.030
Schools and their surrounding
communities are constantly shaping and

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:22.790
being shaped by one another.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:28.760
With Mitchell as our model,
let's zoom out.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.950
Where is the opportunity
gap in American schools?

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:36.715
We know that if high school
graduates who grew up in poverty,

00:02:36.715 --> 00:02:42.610
14% only will graduate
college within eight years.

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.400
We know that third-grade reading
scores are used to predict

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.460
future needed prison capacity.

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:57.525
Now, let's zoom in to our current home,
Palo Alto.

00:02:57.525 --> 00:03:00.140
The green represents wealth.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:04.940
At Duveneck Elementary school,
just 6% of students qualify for free and

00:03:04.940 --> 00:03:08.380
reduced lunch,
a measure of financial need.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:14.788
Students here are 3.6 years above
the national average and they're learning.

00:03:14.788 --> 00:03:21.971
At Los Robles-Ronald McNair Academy,
just one mile away,

00:03:21.971 --> 00:03:27.280
93% of students qualify for
free reduced lunch.

00:03:28.660 --> 00:03:29.250
Students here?

00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:35.350
Students here are nearly three grade
levels below the national average.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:42.960
How is this possible that there is a sixth
grade level gap in our one ZIP code?

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:49.450
Let's talk about funding.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:54.060
When I was a teacher, a rainy day could
mean that I taught verbs while maneuvering

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.780
around a bucket to catch water
dripping through the roof.

00:03:58.930 --> 00:04:00.900
Recess was on a cracked parking lot.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:07.030
These were the physical representations
of a system that provide the fortunate

00:04:07.030 --> 00:04:11.710
with boundless opportunity, while
leaving behind far too many children.

00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:18.490
Across the country, school districts
that serve mostly minority children

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:23.260
receive $23 billion less than
majority white districts.

00:04:24.390 --> 00:04:30.085
In wealthy areas like Palo Alto, property
taxes provide an infusion of money.

00:04:30.085 --> 00:04:36.160
Parent-teacher associations are powerful
and can bring in further funding.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:41.960
We must acknowledge these facts and
their implications.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.620
At the same time,
they're more than just numbers.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:47.250
We the people must get
to know one another.

00:04:48.470 --> 00:04:52.450
At Mitchell, our students brought
the staff homemade tamales.

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:54.288
Sharp around the eighth grade formal,

00:04:54.288 --> 00:05:00.050
arrived at 6.30 in the morning before
at work to conference with teachers.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:07.930
They cared deeply, but they did not always
have the pocket book to meet every need.

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.620
Our time for school is ending soon.

00:05:14.620 --> 00:05:16.880
But our connection to schools is not.

00:05:18.455 --> 00:05:24.490
Today, students are your future
colleagues, employees, and fellow voters.

00:05:26.690 --> 00:05:33.490
The issues in public education are,
yes, messy and bureaucratic.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:35.450
I cannot say they are easy to fix.

00:05:36.650 --> 00:05:38.790
I can say they are not permanent.

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:45.200
Too often, glitzy innovations like
a new education technology tool,

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:47.440
dominate our attention.

00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:50.470
These ideas are great, and they can help.

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:55.200
But the first step is far simpler,
and rooted in our daily lives.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:06:00.770
Figure out how you connect to
the community around you, and respond.

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.410
Mitchell taught me three ways
that we can begin this process.

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:11.360
By thinking about our place,
our resources, and our influence.

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.790
Let's start with where you are,
your place.

00:06:15.790 --> 00:06:20.240
I challenge you to expand
the definition of your community.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.060
Look beyond your familiar blocks.

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:27.290
Learn about the area,
get to know schools like Mitchell.

00:06:27.290 --> 00:06:32.171
If you have children,
send them to public school, don't opt out.

00:06:32.171 --> 00:06:37.410
If you want your kid to be successful in
the real world, send them to school there.

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:40.670
They will benefit from getting to
learn from their classmates, and

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:46.200
being part of that community,
their community.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.970
Secondly, the most tangible, resources.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:54.460
Since the majority of public school
funding comes from property taxes,

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:59.089
schools and property poor neighborhoods
need further access to resources.

00:07:00.350 --> 00:07:04.560
Find a school near you that would benefit
from whatever you can contribute.

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:08.130
That might mean corporate donations or
spare office chairs.

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:13.270
Lastly, how can we use our influence?

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:20.590
The good news is that education
is a unicorn, one of those

00:07:20.590 --> 00:07:25.560
rare areas where members of both
political parties can find agreement.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.220
Across the country, nearly every
school board is publicly elected.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:36.150
Yet not even one in ten people vote
in local school board elections.

00:07:36.150 --> 00:07:41.900
Participate to support candidates who
extend equitable funding to schools and

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:42.520
programs.

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:49.070
I would love if every single
one of you left here today and

00:07:49.070 --> 00:07:52.025
decided to become a middle school teacher.

00:07:52.025 --> 00:07:57.900
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I get it, this is business school.

00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:01.210
No matter where you go
after you leave Stanford,

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:05.870
remember that the brick walls
of a school are not a fortress.

00:08:06.880 --> 00:08:12.030
Our communities will shape our schools and
our schools will shape our communities.

00:08:13.290 --> 00:08:14.580
How will you contribute?

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.230
Roberto is now in eighth grade.

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:23.740
I do not know how his story will unfold.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:32.100
I do know the statistics tell me he
has a 60% of graduating high school.

00:08:32.100 --> 00:08:36.070
He is more likely to become incarcerated
than to graduate from college.

00:08:39.490 --> 00:08:41.532
But I have hope for Roberto.

00:08:41.532 --> 00:08:44.808
I have hope because of
students like Michael,

00:08:44.808 --> 00:08:48.620
Michael was in my first year of teaching.

00:08:48.620 --> 00:08:53.760
He was one of those kids who carried
a second-hand briefcase to school

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:54.665
in sixth grade.

00:08:54.665 --> 00:08:58.280
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Fortunately, Michael was accepted to

00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:02.960
a local scholarship and mentorship program
that supported him through high school.

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:11.960
Michael is now a freshman at Brown,
majoring in neuroscience and education,

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:17.800
because, as Michael told me, he wants to
return and give back to his community.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:23.860
Michael is the young man he is
today because of his hard work,

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:29.440
personal gifts and the support of
a community that carried him forward.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:32.500
With our collective efforts,

00:09:33.690 --> 00:09:37.828
let's make students like Michael
no longer the exception.

00:09:37.828 --> 00:09:39.475
Thank you.

00:09:39.475 --> 00:09:40.678
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:40.678 --> 00:09:45.537
[MUSIC]

